// ===================== S14 · 1. What populism is =====================
m(1,"Definition","According to Müller, populism is…","A moralistic imagination of politics: a pure, unified people against corrupt elites",["A left-wing ideology of economic redistribution in favour of the least advantaged","Any political movement or leader that appeals to “the people” against the powerful","A style of communication based on simple slogans, emotion and a charismatic leader"],"Müller: populism is “a particular moralistic imagination of politics, a way of perceiving the political world that sets a morally pure and fully unified – but ultimately fictional – people against elites who are deemed corrupt or in some other way morally inferior.”");
m(1,"Definition","In Müller's definition, the morally pure and fully unified people that populists invoke is ultimately…","Fictional",["Ethnic","Working-class","Silent"],"The people is “morally pure and fully unified – but, I shall argue, ultimately fictional”.");
m(1,"Anti-elitism","For Müller, being critical of elites is ___ to qualify as a populist.","A necessary but not a sufficient condition",["A sufficient but not a necessary condition","Both a necessary and a sufficient condition","Neither necessary nor sufficient"],"Necessary but not sufficient: otherwise anyone criticizing the powerful and the status quo would by definition be a populist.");
m(1,"Anti-elitism","Why is criticizing elites not enough to make someone a populist?","Otherwise anyone criticizing the powerful would be a populist",["Because populists never criticize elites","Because only right-wing actors criticize elites","Because criticizing elites is illegal in most democracies"],"Anti-elitism is necessary but not sufficient; the populist must also be antipluralist.");
m(1,"Antipluralism","In addition to being antielitist, populists are always…","Antipluralist: they claim that they, and only they, represent the people",["Nationalist: they always defend an ethnic nation","Egalitarian: they always fight for the least advantaged","Anticapitalist: they always attack the market economy"],"“Populists are always antipluralist: populists claim that they, and only they, represent the people.” Müller explicitly says populism is not always nationalism.");
m(1,"Opposition","How do populists treat other political competitors, according to Müller?","As part of the corrupt elite, never as a legitimate opposition",["As legitimate rivals with whom they must compromise","As representatives of other, equally valid parts of the people","As honest but misguided members of the true people"],"Out of power, rivals are “just part of the immoral, corrupt elite”; in government, populists “will not recognize anything like a legitimate opposition”.");
m(1,"Exclusion","What does the populist core claim imply about citizens who do not support populist parties?","They might not be part of the “proper people” to begin with",["They are simply misinformed and must be re-educated","They form a legitimate minority with equal rights","They belong to the “silent majority” the populists also speak for"],"“Whoever does not really support populist parties might not be part of the proper people to begin with.”");
m(1,"Lefort","Which French philosopher said that the supposedly real people first has to be “extracted” from the sum total of actual citizens?","Claude Lefort",["Pierre Bourdieu","Michel Foucault","Alexis de Tocqueville"],"Claude Lefort, French philosopher, cited by Müller.");
m(1,"Lefort","According to Claude Lefort (cited by Müller), the supposedly real people…","First has to be “extracted” from the sum total of actual citizens",["Is identical to the sum total of actual citizens","Is created by the vote of the majority","Exists only in times of revolution"],"The “real people” is a subset “extracted” from all citizens; it is then presumed morally pure and unerring in its will.");
m(1,"The ideal people","Once “extracted”, how is the ideal people presumed to be?","Morally pure and unerring in its will",["Divided and in need of guidance","Corrupt but able to be redeemed","Exploited and politically passive"],"“This ideal people is then presumed to be morally pure and unerring in its will.”");
m(1,"Representative democracy","According to Müller, when does populism arise?","With the introduction of representative democracy",["With the French Revolution and the end of monarchy","With the rise of social media","With the global financial crisis"],"“Populism arises with the introduction of representative democracy; it is its shadow.”");
m(1,"Metaphor","Müller describes populism as the ___ of representative democracy.","Shadow",["Mirror","Cure","Ancestor"],"“Populism arises with the introduction of representative democracy; it is its shadow.”");
m(1,"Rosenblum","Which political theorist called “holism” the notion that the polity should no longer be split?","Nancy Rosenblum",["Hannah Arendt","Chantal Mouffe","Jane Mansbridge"],"Müller borrows “holism” from the political theorist Nancy Rosenblum.");
m(1,"Holism","What is “holism”, the notion populists hanker after (Rosenblum, cited by Müller)?","The idea that the polity should no longer be split and can have one true representative",["The idea that society must be studied as a whole rather than through individuals","The belief that all citizens should vote directly on every law, without representatives","The view that elites and the people should share power equally in every institution"],"Careful: this is not the sociological “holism” of studying society as a whole. Here it means an unsplit polity with one true representative.");
m(1,"Core claim","Müller summarizes the core claim of populism as…","A moralized form of antipluralism",["An economic form of egalitarianism","A cultural form of nationalism","A radical form of direct democracy"],"“The core claim of populism is thus a moralized form of antipluralism. Political actors not committed to this claim are simply not populists.”");
m(1,"Pars pro toto","Populism requires a “pars pro toto” argument. What does that mean?","A part of the people is presented as the whole people",["The whole people is represented by the elected parliament","Each part of society gets a share of power proportional to its size","The leader is only one part of a collective movement"],"Pars pro toto = “a part for the whole”. Along with the claim to exclusive representation, it is understood in a moral, not empirical, sense.");
m(1,"Moral, not empirical","In what sense are the pars pro toto argument and the claim to exclusive representation understood?","In a moral sense, as opposed to an empirical one",["In an empirical sense, based on election results","In a legal sense, based on the constitution","In a statistical sense, based on opinion polls"],"“…with both understood in a moral, as opposed to empirical, sense.”");
m(1,"Necessary condition","According to Müller, there can be no populism without…","Someone speaking for the people as a whole",["A charismatic leader on television","An economic crisis or recession","A referendum on a major issue"],"“There can be no populism, in other words, without someone speaking in the name of the people as a whole.”");
m(1,"Not populists","Political actors who are not committed to the moralized antipluralist claim are, for Müller,…","Simply not populists",["Moderate populists","Left-wing populists","Hidden populists"],"“Political actors not committed to this claim are simply not populists.”");
m(1,"Nietzsche","Müller cites Nietzsche to recall that…","Only that which has no history can be defined",["God is dead","The will to power drives all politics","Morality was invented by the weak"],"“As Nietzsche taught us, only that which has no history can be defined.” Historical languages (and associations of the word populism) are what they are.");
m(1,"Method","Why can a theory of populism not simply follow what movements call themselves?","It may have to exclude some self-declared populists",["Because no movement has ever called itself populist","Because only historians can define populism","Because populism is a purely Latin American phenomenon"],"Theory cannot root itself in one historical experience; we need a theory of the “-ism” to decide which experiences fit.");
m(1,"Analogy","Which analogy does Müller use to show that self-labelling does not decide what counts as populism?","Socialism needn't include National Socialism because Nazis called themselves socialists",["Liberalism must include every party that has “liberal” in its name","Democracy must include every regime calling itself a “people's republic”","Christian democracy is defined by the Christian faith of its own voters"],"Historians would not argue that socialism must include National Socialism because the Nazis called themselves socialists.");
m(1,"People's Party","Which historical template does Müller warn against using as the model for every form of populism?","The American People's Party",["The French Jacobins","The Russian Bolsheviks","The Roman Republic"],"Theory cannot root itself in one historical experience, “with, for example, every form of populism presumed to fit the template of the American People's Party.”");
m(1,"Latin America","Why does a glance at Latin America reinforce the idea that populism must be “popular” (favouring the least advantaged)?","Advocates stressed its inclusionary, emancipatory character in a very unequal continent",["Populist parties there have always been on the far right","Populism there has always been defined by ethnic nationalism","It is the most economically equal continent on the globe"],"Latin America: advocates stressed populism's “inclusionary and emancipatory character in what remains the most economically unequal continent on the globe.”");

vf(1,"For Müller, anyone who criticizes the powerful and the status quo is a populist.",false,"False: criticizing elites is necessary but not sufficient. Populists must also be antipluralist.");
