m(5,"Definition","The science of deliberative democracy studies the capacities of citizens…","As they engage in democratic dialogue",["As isolated individuals answering surveys","Only in social psychology experiments","Only through their votes in elections"],"It seeks evidence on citizens “as they engage democratic dialogue, not as they respond as isolated individuals to survey questions”.");
m(5,"Elements","Which of these is NOT listed as something deliberative democracy incorporates?","Loyalty to a party line",["Mutual justification","Listening and respect","Openness to persuasion"],"It incorporates “inclusive participation that encompasses citizens and leaders, mutual justification, listening, respect, reflection, and openness to persuasion”.");
m(5,"Aristotle","The field of deliberative democracy could be viewed as going as far back as…","Aristotle",["Machiavelli","Rousseau","Tocqueville"],"Aristotle “grounded practical reason in collective political life”.");
m(5,"Aristotle","Aristotle grounded practical reason in…","Collective political life",["Individual meditation","Religious revelation","Market exchange"],"Aristotle grounded practical reason in collective political life.");
m(5,"What's new","What is new in deliberative democracy in the past two decades?","The precision with which its tasks are specified and tested",["The idea that citizens should talk to each other about politics","The invention of free elections with a secret ballot","The use of opinion polls to measure what citizens think"],"New: “the precision with which the tasks of deliberation… have been specified and tested.”");
