m(6,"Algorithms","Which algorithms could help?","Ranking that treats social media as a political sphere, not just social",["Algorithms that maximize engagement and time spent on the platform","Algorithms that hide all political content from users' feeds","Algorithms designed and controlled by the political parties"],"Ranking algorithms that treat social media as a political sphere.");
m(6,"Crowdsourcing","How could crowdsourced judgments of media quality help?","By feeding an algorithm that weights news sources by trustworthiness, to counter misinformation",["By letting users vote to delete the posts of politicians they dislike","By replacing professional journalists with panels of random citizens","By ranking news only by how many people share and like it"],"Ref. 15: Pennycook & Rand.");
m(6,"#Ichbinhier","What does the #Ichbinhier movement in Europe do?","It applies standards of evidence-based argument and civility",["It protests against a new train station in Stuttgart","It runs randomly selected citizens' panels in Oregon","It promotes populist parties and leaders online"],"#Ichbinhier (“I am here”): evidence-based argument and civility.");
m(6,"Margins","Why is there a pressing need to bring deliberative moments in from the margins?","Their value lies in what they do for the whole system",["They are illegal when kept at the margins","They cost nothing when brought to the centre","Only elites attend them at the margins"],"“Because the importance of deliberative moments lies in what they can do for the system as a whole.”");
m(6,"Dáil","What effect did the Irish Convention and Citizens' Assembly have on the Dáil (Irish parliament)?","They made the Dáil's interactions more deliberative",["They replaced it with a citizens' assembly","They made its debates more polarized","They had no effect on the Dáil at all"],"When such processes reach the centre of politics, they helped make the Dáil more deliberative.");
m(6,"Vigilance","Once deliberative moments are brought in from the margins, what must we remain vigilant against?","Governments using them as cover for business as usual",["Citizens becoming too well informed about politics","Too many people wanting to take part in them","Experts refusing to testify before ordinary citizens"],"Risks: symbolic cover for business as usual; lobby groups subverting them and sidelining their recommendations. Practitioners often overcome these problems.");
m(6,"Ireland","According to the Irish case, what can push governments to engage in citizen deliberation?","Faltering trust and public disaffection, to legitimate policy change",["Strong economic growth that gave the state money to experiment","A ruling of the European Court requiring citizens' assemblies","Pressure from the political parties, who wanted to share power"],"“Faltering trust in government and public disaffection can incentivize governments to engage in citizen deliberation in order to legitimate policy change.”");
m(6,"Stuttgart 21","What was “Stuttgart 21”?","A project to rebuild Stuttgart's central train station",["A citizens' assembly made up of 21 randomly selected members","A German populist party founded in Stuttgart in 2021","A 2021 law making referendums compulsory in Germany"],"Massive societal protests can induce governments to offer citizen dialogues.");
m(6,"Stuttgart 21","What did the conflicts around Stuttgart 21 lead to?","Compulsory citizen deliberation for big infrastructure in Baden-Württemberg",["The cancellation of all large infrastructure projects in Germany","A national referendum on the future of German railways","The creation of a Citizens' Initiative Review on the Oregon model"],"Sustainable deliberative innovation: compulsory citizen deliberation for large infrastructure projects in Baden-Württemberg.");
m(6,"India & China","Facing failures to solve social problems or cope with economic development, governments sometimes…","Constitutionalize (India) or promote (China) local deliberative exercises",["Promote (India) or constitutionalize (China) local deliberative exercises","Ban local deliberation (India and China)","Replace elections with deliberation (India and China)"],"India constitutionalizes, China promotes.");
m(6,"Spontaneous?","Does deliberative development often happen spontaneously?","No, it is rare",["Yes, almost always","Only in liberal democracies","Only during crises"],"“It is rare that deliberative development happens spontaneously in such cases.”");
m(6,"Benign deployment","When are the prospects for benign deployment of deliberation good?","When practitioners already have ties with leaders and publics",["When practitioners are called in desperation during a crisis","When governments act alone, without scholars or practitioners","When deliberation stays at the margins of political life"],"Examples: the Irish constitutional convention and Healthy Democracy Oregon.");
m(6,"Healthy Democracy","Which organization runs the Oregon review process?","Healthy Democracy Oregon",["Participedia","Common Cause","Everyday Democracy"],"Healthy Democracy Oregon. Common Cause and Everyday Democracy only appear in the authors' competing-interests note.");
m(6,"Conclusion","What is the article's conclusion?","Citizens can deliberate well, but it won't happen on its own",["Citizens can't deliberate, so experts must govern","Deliberation will spread on its own through social media","Representative democracy must be abolished"],"“The citizenry is quite capable of sound deliberation. But deliberative democratization will not just happen.”");
m(6,"Reconstruction","How should the political reconstruction ideally be carried out?","Deliberatively and democratically, with citizens and leaders",["By social scientists and experts alone","By a strong leader with a clear mandate","By the courts, through constitutional rulings"],"“That political reconstruction itself would ideally be deliberative and democratic.”");

