// [part, category, c=concept / p=person, term, definition, accepted variants]
// Categories keep populism (pop) and deliberation (delib) apart, so generated wrong options stay clearly wrong.
// ===================== More questions, with a difficulty (d: 1 easy, 2 medium, 3 hard) =====================
// mostly applied cases: does this example count as populism for Müller? what would deliberation research expect?
const lv=(d,f,...a)=>{f(...a);const L=f===ty?TQ:Q;L[L.length-1].d=d};
// ---- S14 · 1. What populism is
lv(3,m,1,"Case","A politician says: “The establishment is corrupt, but my opponents are honest citizens with other views and have every right to govern if they win.” Is this populism for Müller?","No: it is critical of elites but accepts pluralism",["Yes: any criticism of the elite is populist","Yes: it uses the word “establishment”","No: because populism must be left-wing"],"Anti-elitism is necessary but not sufficient. Populists are always antipluralist: they deny their rivals' legitimacy.");
lv(3,m,1,"Case","A leader says: “We, and only we, are the real people. Whoever votes against us betrays the nation.” What makes this populist?","The moralized antipluralist claim to exclusive representation",["The use of the word “nation”","The criticism of voters","The tone of the speech"],"Populism = a pars pro toto argument plus an exclusive claim to represent the people, in a moral rather than an empirical sense.");
lv(3,m,1,"Case","A party wins 40% of the vote and says: “Our mandate is democratic: we speak for 40% of voters, and others speak for the rest.” For Müller, this speaker is…","Not a populist: they accept being only a part of a plural people",["A populist, because they claim a mandate","A populist, because they mention voters","A populist, because they are antielitist"],"Populists claim to represent the whole people in a moral sense; recognising other legitimate representatives is pluralism.");
lv(3,m,1,"Case","Why would Müller say that a populist who wins 60% of the vote is still not making an empirical claim?","Their claim is moral: only they are the real people, whatever the polls say",["Because votes never count in populism","Because they win by cheating","Because 60% is not enough"],"The pars pro toto argument and the exclusive-representation claim are moral, not empirical, claims.");
lv(2,m,1,"Case","A Leninist vanguard party rejects pluralism and claims to know the true interests of the working class. Why isn't it populist for Müller?","Because it does not see the people as morally pure and unerring",["Because it is left-wing","Because it accepts pluralism","Because it criticises elites"],"Antipluralism is necessary but not sufficient: only populists see the people as morally pure and unerring.");
lv(2,m,1,"Definition","In what sense is populism the “shadow of representative democracy”?","It arises along with representative democracy, as a permanent possibility within it",["It appears only after democracy collapses","It exists only in dictatorships","It replaces representation by direct rule"],"Populism arises with the introduction of representative democracy.");
lv(3,m,1,"Method","Why does Müller refuse to define populism simply by what movements call themselves?","Self-labelling doesn't decide: Nazis called themselves socialists, yet socialism needn't include National Socialism",["Because movements never use the word populism","Because definitions should be decided by the majority","Because populist movements do not exist"],"A good theory may exclude some self-declared populists.");
lv(3,m,1,"Method","What is the point of Müller's warning against using the American People's Party as the template?","Not every populism looks like a farmers' movement favouring the least advantaged",["That American populism was not real populism","That populism is always agrarian","That Latin America has no populism"],"He warns against treating one historical template as the model for every form of populism.");
lv(1,vf,1,"Müller says populists claim that they, and only they, represent the people.",true,"This is the antipluralist core.");
lv(2,vf,1,"A political actor who criticises elites but accepts that opponents are legitimate is a populist for Müller.",false,"Anti-elitism alone is not sufficient.");
lv(3,vf,1,"Müller treats populism as a pathology that appears only when representative democracy is absent.",false,"It arises with representative democracy: it is its shadow.");
lv(2,ty,1,"Latin phrase for taking a part as the whole",["pars pro toto"],"Populism requires a pars pro toto argument.");
lv(3,ty,1,"Müller's summary of populism's core claim: a moralized form of ___",["antipluralism"],"Antielitist and antipluralist.");
// ---- S14 · 2. Who is "the people"?
lv(3,m,2,"Case","A politician says: “Only the real people voted for us; the other half of the country doesn't count.” For Müller this is…","The core claim of populism: only some of the people are really the people",["Just a normal election slogan","An empirical statement about turnout","A defence of the common people against elites"],"Farage's “victory for real people” and Trump's “the other people don't mean anything” follow this logic.");
lv(3,m,2,"Case","An activist defends the poor, the excluded and the forgotten and denounces the powerful. Is that enough to call them a populist?","No: defending “the common people” isn't enough without the exclusive moral claim",["Yes: the common people are the people","Yes: any activist against elites is populist","No: because activists are never populists"],"One sense of “the people” is the common people, but advocating for them is insufficient evidence of populism.");
lv(2,m,2,"Concepts","Which of the three senses of “the people” refers to all members of the polity?","The body politic",["The common people","The nation in a cultural sense","The voters of one party"],"Müller's three senses: the people as a whole (body politic), the common people, and the nation (cultural).");
lv(3,m,2,"Case","Which statement in a speech would count as populism in terms of ancient Rome?","“Only the plebs is the real populus Romanus”",["“The plebs deserve better laws.”","“The Senate should meet more often.”","“All Romans, patricians and plebs, belong to Rome.”"],"Claiming that the plebs alone is the whole populus = pars pro toto; fighting for the plebs' interests isn't populism by itself.");
lv(3,m,2,"Case","Which statement in Machiavelli's Florence would count as populism?","“The grandi don't belong in Florence, whatever they do.”",["“The popolo deserves more power.”","“The grandi should pay taxes.”","“Florence needs a new magistrate.”"],"Saying a group is outside the people whatever it does = exclusion of part of the polity.");
lv(3,m,2,"Wallace","Müller says Wallace's “segregation forever” speech was populist, not only racist, because…","He claimed to speak in the name of “the greatest people” alone, excluding those who opposed him",["He defended segregation","He criticised the Kennedy administration","He was governor of Alabama"],"The populist element lay in the exclusive claim to speak for the (real) people.");
lv(2,m,2,"Idealisation","Is idealising the people (as Bakunin or the narodniki did) automatically populism for Müller?","Not necessarily",["Always","Only in the nineteenth century","Only when done by a government"],"An idealization of the people is not by itself populism in his sense.");
lv(1,vf,2,"For Müller, every appeal to “the people” is populism.",false,"Populism requires an exclusive, moral claim to be the people.");
lv(2,vf,2,"Trump's remark about the “other people” who “don't mean anything” is an example of the populist logic.",true,"Only some of the people are really the people.");
lv(3,vf,2,"Farage's statement implied that the 48% who voted Remain were not proper members of the political community.",true,"Müller: it made them “less than real”.");
lv(2,ty,2,"Müller's term for the people as the whole, all members of the polity",["body politic"],"Opposed to the common people and the nation.");
// ---- S14 · 3. Work, ethnicity & other antipluralists
lv(3,m,3,"Case","A movement claims that “hardworking citizens” are being exploited by corrupt politicians above and benefit-takers below. Which concept applies?","Producerism",["Holism","Epistocracy","Cultural cognition"],"Producerism pits the pure, hardworking people against those who do not really work.");
lv(3,m,3,"Case","Which situation shows Müller's claim that the criterion for “the pure people” need not be work?","Ethnic markers replace “work” as the criterion, e.g. racist ideas about “welfare queens”",["A movement that defends only workers","A leader who ignores minorities","A party that rejects all criteria"],"If “work” is indeterminate, ethnic markers can replace it, but populism is not always nationalism or ethnic chauvinism.");
lv(3,m,3,"Case","Right-wing populists often claim a symbiosis between…","A corrupt elite above and marginal groups below",["Workers and employers","The state and the church","Citizens and immigrants against the army"],"Both are portrayed as not belonging to the pure people.");
lv(3,m,3,"Case","Which example shows right-wing populist logic in an “almost ridiculously obvious and literal” way?","The claim that Obama was both a “bicoastal elite” member and an African American Other",["Berlusconi's media empire","Wallace's inauguration speech","The Brexit referendum"],"The birthers wanted to prove he was an illegal, “un-American” usurper.");
lv(2,vf,3,"Müller thinks populism is always a form of ethnic nationalism.",false,"There are many ways to distinguish the moral from the immoral.");
lv(2,vf,3,"Intolerant religious actors can be antipluralist without being populist.",true,"Only populists see the people as morally pure and unerring.");
