lv(3,m,4,"Case","Citizens are exposed to thousands of opinions online and mostly follow cues from their own party. Which problem does Dryzek et al. name?","Too many opportunities for expression make it hard to detect the signal amid the noise",["A lack of opportunities to express views","Too few media outlets","A shortage of political parties"],"Counterintuitively, the overabundance of expression is a problem for democracy.");
lv(3,m,4,"Case","Why is it “futile” to ask elites to behave better?","Because the public stays ripe to be polarized by demagogues",["Because elites never listen","Because elites behave perfectly already","Because voters don't care about elites"],"Uncivil elites and pathological mass communication reinforce each other, so ordinary citizens must be part of any response.");
lv(3,m,4,"Case","Someone refuses to accept evidence on climate change because it threatens their group's identity. Which concept is this?","Cultural cognition",["Epistocracy","Deliberation","Holism"],"A felt truth in which social identity shapes opinion, seen clearly on climate change.");
lv(3,m,4,"Critique","Why do the authors call survey research on citizen competence “monological”?","It studies individuals reasoning in isolation, not people reasoning together",["It only uses one language","It only studies elites","It uses a single question"],"Psychological research shows that bad solitary reasoners can be good group problem-solvers.");
lv(3,m,4,"Critique","Which political regime is encouraged by claims that people vote mainly by group identity?","An “epistocracy” of wise elites",["A deliberative democracy","A direct democracy","A federation"],"Achen & Bartels-type claims can fuel epistocratic arguments.");
